1484375, 0.0152740478515625, -0.002933502197265625, -0.014129638671875, 0.01158905029296875, -0.00832366943359375, 0.02484130859375, -0.023406982421875, -0.00498199462890625, -0.009002685546875, -0.029052734375, -0.00799560546875, -0.01457977294921875, -0.01026153564453125, 0.005466461181640625, 0.0396728515625, 0.019012451171875, 0.0031337738037109375, -0.0202484130859375, 0.0063629150390625, 0.0085296630859375, 0.004058837890625, 0.006137847900390625, 0.00013566017150878906, -0.0128631591796875, 2.2709369659423828e-05, -0.0299530029296875, -0.00756072998046875, -0.01206207275390625, -0.0265350341796875, 0.0243072509765625, -0.042694091796875, -0.0009436607360839844, -0.0256195068359375, -0.0103759765625, 0.02783203125, -0.028778076171875, -0.02081298828125, -0.00890350341796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that incrementally learn from a stream of tasks and update a textual memory bank to improve over time\u2014have recently shown strong results. However, their *reliability aspects*, specifically the consistency and robustness of their performance across different stochasticities and task sequences, have not been thoroughly explored. In this work, we address this gap by re-evaluating two representative memory-based methods: (a) episodic memory augmentation for language agents, and (b) reinforcement learning agents with retrieved textual archives. Both methods operate by updating a persistent memory after each task, which is later referenced during subsequent decision-making.\n\nOur methodology aims to expose potential *fragilities*\u2014that is, the susceptibility of these agents to performance drops or erratic behavior under variations such as environment stochasticity and task order. We conduct multiple independent runs (ten per configuration) to capture variance, and systematically randomize task sequences rather than adopting a fixed order. Tasks encompass both procedural and reasoning-heavy challenges in simulated multi-step environments, evaluated using task completion rates and average episode reward.\n\nOur findings reveal two primary weaknesses in current approaches. First, agent evaluation exhibits high variance: in the same conditions, measured performance differs significantly between runs (with standard deviations up to 15% in completion rates), and the addition of a self-improving loop can magnify this unpredictability, amplifying subtle errors in memory updates. Second, improvement trajectories are highly sensitive to task order. When tasks are not carefully ordered, average agent gains drop, and in many runs improvement is entirely absent. We attribute this to an *implicit curriculum*: previous studies select task orders that unintentionally scaffold learning, while more challenging, unordered setups expose failures.\n\nTo probe the source of this fragility, we manually inspect agent memory traces and observe many errors tied to ambiguous or underspecified tasks and feedback. We hypothesize that the lack of explicit guidance or structured feedback leaves agents vulnerable to misinterpretation and memory drift. To evaluate this hypothesis, we augment agent memory construction by integrating (i) detailed rubrics clarifying task requirements, and (ii) explicit, programmatic environment feedback on partial success and failure states. These enhancements yield consistent but partial performance improvements (raising worst-case completion rates by 5\u20138%), yet notable unpredictability and sensitivity to task order persist, indicating that underspecification is only one of several contributing factors. Remaining gaps include residual high variance and unrecoverable agent failures in random orderings, revealing a need to address additional sources of instability.\n\nBased on our findings, we recommend (1) that future evaluations of self-improving agents include reporting both mean and variance across multiple random seeds and task orderings; (2) that benchmark protocols should minimize implicit curricula by varying task sequences and including stress-test splits; and (3) that interfaces explicitly support specification\u2014such as rubrics and environment-provided signals\u2014for both agent comprehension and human oversight, allowing intervention or diagnosis when memory-based improvements fail. We hope these actionable recommendations will encourage the development of more robust, transparent, and trustworthy self-improving agents."}}